isemitism and various forms of bigotry. Established against the backdrop of rising prejudice and discrimination, particularly against Jewish communities, the ADL was instrumental in advocating for civil rights and social justice, positioning itself as a significant player in the broader struggle against intolerance in the United States. The organization was founded by Sigmund Livingston, who sought to address anti-Jewish violence and discrimination, notably after the infamous Leo Frank case, which highlighted the dangers of societal prejudice.

From its inception, the ADL focused on education, advocacy, and legal efforts to combat hate and discrimination. It initiated various programs, including the publication of educational materials, the development of the “Pyramid of Hate” model, and participation in legal actions to promote civil rights, culminating in landmark cases such as the 1964 Civil Rights Act. The ADL also played a crucial role in monitoring and combating hate groups across the nation, influencing legislation and public policy related to discrimination and hate crimes.

